/usr/examples -- small programs to read, run and change

Copy one to your own directory first:  cp /usr/examples/hello.c .

  hello.c         the classic first C program
                    cc hello.c; ./a.out
  multitask.c     several processes at once: workers that never yield,
                  interleaved by the timer (preemption), each with its own
                  memory, results through a pipe, exit statuses by wait()
                    cc -o multitask multitask.c; ./multitask [workers]
  draw.c          a picture on the video card's screen: lines, boxes, a
                  filled block, text, through /dev/video (man 4 video);
                  the console's text comes back when it ends
                    cc -o draw draw.c; ./draw
  clock.c         a clock, drawn with the graphics library (man gfx):
                  circles, lines, text, and hands that move by being
                  drawn twice in the ink that turns dots over
                    cc -o clock clock.c -lgfx; ./clock
  mouse.c         the mouse: a cross that follows it, the left button
                  draws, the right one ends (man gfx, man 4 mouse)
                    cc -o mouse mouse.c -lgfx; ./mouse
  wm.hand         a hand for the window manager to play: two terminals,
                  a command in each, one dragged, one closed (man wm)
                    wm -s wm.hand
  people.c        C and the SQL library: reads the "shop" database
                  that inventory.bas makes (before its last line drops it)
                    cc -o people people.c -lsql; ./people
  inventory.bas   BASIC and SQL: a table made, filled, queried, an SQL
                  error caught with ON ERROR
                    basic inventory.bas
  plot.bas        BASIC's graphics: a curve with its axes and labels,
                  a ball of ellipses, on the video card's screen
                    basic plot.bas
  primes.bas      BASIC: the primes below 1000 (a sieve, PRINT USING)
                    basic primes.bas
  guess.bas       BASIC: a guessing game (INPUT, RND)
                    basic guess.bas
  count.sh        a shell script: for, expr, if / test
                    sh count.sh one two three

More come as the system grows. The manual explains each language and
command: man cc, man basic, man sql, man sh (man intro for the rest).
